  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=4$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ | ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $2\\pi $ | $-3$ | $\\pi $ | $5$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=4.$",
  "worked_solution": "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))=f(x){g}^{\u2032}(x)+g(x){f}^{\u2032}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=4,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=4} & =f(4){g}^{\u2032}(4)+g(4){f}^{\u2032}(4) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 5+(-3)\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =7\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
